{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 3 and 6 distinguish the documented scoring procedure from section 9 evidence of precision. One score without execution history establishes neither repeated evaluation nor instability; repeatability evidence is insufficient to rate.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Whether repeated evaluations occurred is unknown."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "One score provides no evidence of variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA section 9, the execution record establishes that repeatability was not studied. This absence supplies no evidence of instability and does not justify an inadequate precision rating.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The record explicitly excludes repeated evaluation."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "A single evaluation cannot demonstrate instability across evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7 are interpreted as consistency across repeated AI evaluations. The observed range is two percentage points, satisfying the report's three-point rule. This local rule is not an EFPA rating anchor; broader precision remains unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations with matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The two-point range is within the permitted maximum of three points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "Reinterpreting EFPA section 9 for AI repetitions, the observed range is 31 percentage points and fails the report's stability rule. This establishes a consistency problem for this comparison; it does not determine precision in other conditions or an EFPA coefficient rating.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations under matching conditions are explicit."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The 31-point range exceeds the stated three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 6.2–6.3 address the topic and scoring documentation. Sections 6.1 and 9 separately require evidence about item quality and precision. Neither answer mistakes nor repeated evaluation is established by the supplied document.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The supplied material does not establish whether repetitions were studied."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "No evidence demonstrating answer mistakes is supplied."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "The appendix adds information relevant to EFPA 6.3.8 software requirements and support. It adds no evidence for section 9 precision or section 6.1 answer quality; documentation breadth cannot substitute for those findings.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Installation information does not establish repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results or examination of answers",
          "reason": "The appendix supplies no findings demonstrating answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.1.3 supports recording independent content review during development, but that process does not establish released-answer correctness. Pre-reconciliation agreement is unavailable; reconciliation alone cannot supply an agreement coefficient. Section 9.6 would apply only if judgement-based scoring requires it.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release",
          "reason": "Both components received independent developmental examination."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "The review process does not establish that every released answer is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.1.3 records the content-review procedure, while sections 6.1 and 7 require attention to the twelve wrong released answers. Their existence establishes a content defect despite prior review. Its effect on scores and intended interpretations remains unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release",
          "reason": "Both questions and answers were examined during development."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "Explicitly identified wrong answers disprove universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.3.2 distinguishes automated scoring consistency from evidence that scoring is correct. Deterministic marks establish only consistency for identical text. Section 9.6 is inapplicable to the described fixed, nonjudgemental rule; independent scoring accuracy remains unestablished.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported",
          "reason": "The passage does not establish that such testing occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "A repeatable mark need not be correct, and no universal accuracy evidence is provided."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA 6.3.2, comparison with independently established marks directly examines scoring correctness. Two unresolved discrepancies establish scoring errors in the tested responses. The 20 chosen responses do not establish accuracy elsewhere; section 9.6 is inapplicable to the fixed rule.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The required independent comparison is explicitly reported."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved",
          "reason": "The reported discrepancies conflict with correctness for every response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 10.5 evaluates evidence for the stated interpretation. The counts support the narrowly defined comparison in this evaluation. EFPA 8.3.2.2 permits a norms exception for an explicitly restricted raw-score ranking purpose; this supplies no broader generalisation.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly answer this restricted question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24",
          "reason": "A solved twelve more evaluated problems under the reported conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 6.1 and 10 require a rationale and validity evidence matching the breadth of the intended interpretation. These grid results support a local comparison but do not establish general intelligence across subjects and settings. Broad validity is unestablished rather than disproved.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied",
          "reason": "Evidence connecting this narrow comparison to the broad stated use is missing."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24",
          "reason": "The counts establish A's advantage on the evaluated problems."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 3, 6.1.3.1 and 10 assess scoring against its declared purpose. The binary rule matches the requirement to count completely correct grids. Excluding partial credit is therefore consistent with this use; implementation accuracy and precision are separate unknowns.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "One point for a completely correct grid and zero otherwise matches the requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for partial correctness follows the stated exclusion."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 3 and 6.1.3.1 reveal an explicit mismatch between scoring and the stated requirement, relevant to section 10 intended-use validity. The rule discards required cell-level credit. This is an evidenced defect, unlike a mere documentation gap.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell",
          "reason": "The binary rule withholds required credit from partially correct grids."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A partially correct grid receives zero",
          "reason": "Zero credit conflicts with the requirement to credit correctly completed cells in partial outputs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 2.8 and 9 distinguish repeated identical conditions from changed administration. Section 10.3.1.6 can accommodate the instruction comparison: it examines scores as wording changes. Without identical-instruction repetitions, instruction effects cannot be separated fully from repeat variability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction",
          "reason": "Instruction wording changes, so conditions are not identical."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study measures score changes while varying instructions and holding the listed other conditions fixed; isolated causal attribution remains uncertain."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "Reinterpreting EFPA section 9 for AI evaluations, the study examines consistency with all listed conditions fixed. No numerical results are supplied, so the degree of precision cannot be rated. EFPA 10.3.1.6 provides no instruction-change evidence because instructions never vary.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported score changes occur across evaluations with matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied",
          "reason": "The study explicitly excludes the variation needed to examine changed instructions."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No clarification was required. The complete original EFPA model supplied the framework: Part A description, Part B sections 6–12 and final evaluation, and Part C source attribution. These are focused applications, as requested, rather than full questionnaires. Test taker was reinterpreted as AI system and repeated testing as repeated execution. Human bodily capabilities, participant debriefing and human demographic accommodations are inapplicable to the AI itself; operator guidance and relevant comparison-group fairness remain separate questions. Unaddressed documentation, materials, norms, precision, validity, fair-use and generated-report properties remain unknown, not automatically inadequate or inapplicable. Under the original scale, insufficient applicable evidence corresponds to 0, not 1; no numerical ratings or aggregate score were calculated. Critical 0 or 1 ratings, if formally assigned, would invoke EFPA's restricted-use recommendation. Source: EFPA Test Review Model, version 5.0, August 2025, supplied extraction, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://www.efpa.eu/. This application implies no EFPA endorsement."
}
